    "category": "provenance",
    "short_definition": "Reliability class of a source, from Tier 1 (strongest) to Tier 4 (weakest).",
    "full_definition": "Every source in the registry is classed by the kind of evidence it provides. Tier 1 is primary, official or licensed data (SEC filings, FRED, licensed price feeds). Tier 2 is professional media and licensed research. Tier 3 is specialist or social commentary. Tier 4 is anonymous or unverified material. Each stored observation keeps a snapshot of its source's tier.",
    "formula": "Tier 1 primary official/issuer/licensed feed; Tier 2 professional media/licensed data; Tier 3 expert/social/specialist; Tier 4 unverified",
    "unit": "label (Tier 1 to Tier 4)",
    "example": "SEC EDGAR is Tier 1; an anonymous repost is Tier 4.",
    "direction": "Lower tier number = stronger evidence",
    "interpretation": "Trade-critical facts need Tier 1 confirmation; Tier 3 and 4 are leads, never sole evidence.",
    "why_it_matters": "Weights evidence and blocks weak-source-only signals.",
    "source": "Research report 01 §20 (Data Source Hierarchy); errata E2",
